## 7. CYBER LABS — FREE ACCESS AND OPTIONAL VERIFICATION

Cyber Labs must be free for every student.

Remove:
- Paid lab restrictions.
- Premium lab labels.
- Payment verification.
- Subscription checks.
- Purchase requirements.
- Payment-based access controls.

### Lab verification configuration

Add an individual setting for every lab:

Completion Verification Required: YES / NO

When NO:
- Students can access and practise without a verification token.
- No static lab verification token is required to open the lab.
- Completion follows the configured learning and assessment rules.
- Do not automatically award completion merely because the student opened the lab.

When YES:
- Use appropriate completion verification.
- CTF labs can use flag submission.
- Isolated execution labs can use runner-confirmed completion.
- Other labs can use suitable task-based assessments.

Verification must never be confused with payment.

### Lab access without MicroVM infrastructure

Allow educational content, walkthroughs, simulated terminal exercises and suitable practice labs to work without an external MicroVM runner.

Only require isolated execution for labs that genuinely need it.

If the external runner is unavailable:
- Show a clear message.
- Do not falsely claim a lab is running.
- Keep non-runner-dependent learning content accessible.
- Do not unnecessarily block every lab.

### Critical XP and verification security fixes

Audit the existing lab verification implementation.

The previous audit identified that the existing /verify endpoint may permit completion without an active runner session and repeated XP farming.

Fix these vulnerabilities:
- Verify session ownership.
- Verify appropriate completion conditions.
- Prevent proof replay.
- Award XP only once per eligible completion.
- Use transactional database operations.
- Preserve existing completed records.
- Backfill historical XP award state safely.
- Prevent repeated completion submissions from increasing XP.
- Never trust frontend-only completion status.

Do not deploy or claim an external runner is operational until it is actually connected and tested.

